# swell: a flat chequer that computes its own bulge
#
#     $ vonal trace examples/swell.vsr swell.gif --max-steps 14000
#
# The plate ships flat. Every square in rows 5 to 19 is the same size, a
# regular lattice with nothing to look at. The program walks all 225 of them
# and writes a radial falloff into their scales, so the lattice swells into a
# sphere while it runs. examples/swell.gif is that inflation.
#
# This inverts orb. There the swell is authored into the scales and the
# program ignores it. Here the swell is what the program computes, and the
# picture that ships is the one from before the idea arrived.
#
# The falloff is
#
#     scale = 7 - (dx*dx + dy*dy) / 14,  dx = x - 7,  dy = y - 7
#
# and two things about it belong to this language rather than merely running
# in it. dx*dx is never negative, so no absolute value is needed, and an abs
# would cost a comparison, which in a 2D grid is a physical detour. And the
# range is exact: the corner is dx*dx + dy*dy = 98, and 98/14 = 7, so scale
# lands in 0 to 7 by construction. PUT rejects anything outside that range as
# a runtime fault, so a formula that cannot leave it is the difference between
# a loop body of straight-line arithmetic and one threaded around two
# branches. This body has no branch at all. The only control flow is the loop.
#
# One counter does it: k runs 0 to 224, x = k mod 15 and y = k / 15, which is
# the trick bubble uses for its passes. push 3 / roll / push 5 is the whole
# address calculation. ROLL rotates [x, y, s] into [s, x, y], which is the
# order PUT pops them in, and 5 is the row the composition starts on.
#
# The body is 37 cells and a row is 15, so the loop is a cycle through rows 1
# to 4 rather than one wrapping row. East along row 1, south at column 14,
# west along row 2, south at column 1, east along row 3, south at column 14,
# west along row 4, then up column 0 and back to the start. The turns that are
# not corners are no-ops: a turn that re-states the heading the eye already
# has costs a step and nothing else, which is what the spare cells hold.
#
# k is seeded at -1 because the increment sits at the top of the cycle rather
# than the bottom. The test at (13,1) turns north when k reaches 225, onto the
# void at (13,0) -- the one cell here that draws nothing, placed in the
# program band where the composition does not miss it.
#
# Every square in the field is ADD. Only the ground alternates, and because
# the variant is an offset rather than a colour the figure alternates with it:
# cream on black, cobalt on cream. One instruction, two colours, which is what
# the offset encoding was for. The ground has no semantics anywhere, so the
# same chequer runs under the program band, and the code shows only as the
# glyphs it needs.
%plate 15x20

o11.  #631  v13.  #131  #13.  #131  #13.  #131  #13.  #131  #13.  #131  #13.  ...1  #13.
>131  >13.  >131  o11.  #131  %13.  o131  o24.  o211  D33.  >131  >13.  >131  ^23.  v131
^13.  v131  %13.  #231  o17.  %431  #43.  o271  o11.  %431  #53.  o271  o11.  %131  <13.
^131  >13.  #331  %43.  o171  #23.  %131  #33.  #131  o11.  o261  #43.  o171  %33.  v131
^13.  <131  <13.  <131  <13.  <131  <13.  <131  @23.  #131  o15.  %531  o13.  #231  <13.
